  "Question": "If \\(\\frac{x}{x-1}=\\frac{y^2+2y-1}{y^2+2y-2},\\) then \\(x\\) equals",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(y^2+2y-1\\)",
    "B": "\\(y^2+2y-2\\)",
    "C": "\\(y^2+2y+2\\)",
    "D": "\\(y^2+2y+1\\)",
    "E": "\\(-y^2-2y+1\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "Each denominator is one less than its numerator. Let \\(T=y^2+2y-1\\), so the equation becomes\n\\[\n\\frac{x}{x-1}=\\frac{T}{T-1}.\n\\]\nThe original fractions require \\(x\\ne1\\) and \\(T\\ne1\\). We may therefore multiply by \\((x-1)(T-1)\\). Expanding reveals a common product that cancels:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(T-1)&=T(x-1),\\\\\nxT-x&=xT-T,\\\\\nx&=T.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nFor this value, \\(x-1=T-1\\ne0\\), and the original fractions are identical, so no extraneous value has been introduced. Therefore\n\\[\nx=y^2+2y-1,\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\), whenever the original equation is defined.",
